Done. Both the package change and the one producer that feeds it.

**`internal/decision/record.go`**
- `Failures []string` added to `Record`, emitted as one `--related-failure` per non-empty entry, alongside the existing `--related-invariant` loop.
- `ErrNotLinked` now says "an invariant or a failure mode", and `Validate` requires one of those two — source files alone no longer satisfy it.
- A `links` helper shared by `Validate` and `Args`, so a whitespace-only id is read the same way by both. Without it, `Invariants: []string{"  "}` would pass validation but emit no flag — the exact bug being fixed, one level down.
- `Write` already validated before `exec.LookPath`, so the unlinked case returns `ErrNotLinked` with no CLI invocation; a test now pins that ordering against a stub `sensei` that fails if run.
- Missing title/rationale still returns its own plain error, not `ErrNotLinked`.

**`internal/workflow/engine.go`** — the architect is the only producer of these links, so I wired `related_failures` through `architectureDecision` and its prompt schema. Without this the new field would be unreachable in production and file-only decisions would silently stop recording. The prompt also now states that a decision is recorded only if linked to a real invariant or failure mode, keeping the existing "do not invent an id" instruction.

I confirmed the premise and the fix against the real CLI with `--dry-run`: a decision carrying only `--source-file` is rejected with *"contract-first: link a related_invariant or related_failure"*, while one carrying only `--related-failure` validates and appends as `mitigates:`.

`gofmt`, `go vet ./...`, and `go test ./...` all pass; `internal/decision` gained `record_test.go` covering the accept/refuse cases above.

One thing I did **not** do: file this as a Sensei scar with `sensei propose`. This repo has no `docs/awareness/` directory, and the default `--target-repo` is the separate awareness-graph repo — either target means writing outside the bounded candidate, which isn't mine to do. Worth a follow-up from you if you want the failure class recorded.

